        <h1>Exercise Name: Standing Bent-Over Low-Cable Row (Close Underhand Grip)</h1>
        <u><b><h4>Exercise Overview</h4></b></u>

<pre>This 3D anatomical visualization demonstrates Standing Bent-Over Low-Cable Rows (Close Underhand / Supinated Grip)
     viewed from a rear quarter angle. The video highlights the lower and middle back muscles—specifically 
     the lower lats (Latissimus Dorsi)—in glowing orange-red and yellow colors, illustrating how pulling a 
     cable bar toward your waist with an underhand grip targets the lower lat fibers.

 Exercise Name: Standing Bent-Over Low-Cable Row (Close Underhand Grip)
 Target Muscles: Lower Lats (Latissimus Dorsi — lower fibers), Middle Back (Rhomboids, Lower Trapezius)
 Secondary Muscles: Biceps (Biceps Brachii), Brachialis, Forearms, Erector Spinae (lower back stabilization), Glutes & Hamstrings
</pre>

</div></div>

<h1>
    
    How to Perform (Step-by-Step)</h1><u><b><h4>1. Setup</h4></b></u>: Attach a short straight bar to the lowest pulley of a cable machine. Stand facing the machine, hinge forward at your hips while keeping your back flat and knees slightly soft. Grab the bar with an underhand grip (palms facing upward) about shoulder-width apart.
 <u><b><h4>2. Pulling Phase (Concentric)</h4></b></u>: Exhale as you pull the cable bar upward and backward toward your lower belly/navel. Drive your elbows close to your sides and push them back behind your torso.
 <u><b><h4>3. Peak Squeeze</h4></b></u>: Pause for a split second at the peak position as the bar reaches your torso, squeezing your lower lats together.
 <u><b><h4>4. Return Phase (Eccentric)</h4></b></u>: Inhale as you slowly extend your arms back out toward the low pulley under full control until you feel a complete stretch in your lower back and lats.


<h1>Key Benefits</h1>

 <b>◉Targeted Lower Lat Activation:</b> The supinated underhand grip allows for a greater elbow sweep along the sides of your body, directly isolating the lower fibers of the lats.<br>

 <b>◉Increased Bicep Leverage:</b> Underhand positioning allows the biceps to assist more efficiently, enabling you to lift heavier loads safely while keeping tension on the back.<br>

 <b>◉Constant Cable Tension:</b> Unlike free-weight barbell rows where tension drops off at certain angles, cables deliver smooth, continuous resistance across the entire movement.


<h1>Recommended Sets & Reps</h1>

| Goal | Sets | Reps | Rest |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Muscle Building (Hypertrophy) | 3 – 4 | 10 – 12 | 60 – 90 seconds |
| Endurance / Tone | 3 – 4 | 12 – 15 | 45 – 60 seconds |
| Strength | 4 – 5 | 6 – 8 | 2 minutes |

Keep your chest up and spine neutral throughout every repetition—avoid rounding your spine or using torso bouncing to pull the weight.

